---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE, STATUS, href } from "../lib/catalog.js";
---
<Base title="About" description="What Quitanda is, how listings are chosen and checked, and who runs it." section="about">
  <main class="wrap page narrow">
    <article class="prose">
      <header><h1>About Quitanda</h1></header>

      <p>Quitanda is a catalog of everything that gives a click-wheel iPod a second life: custom firmware, the apps that manage its music, and the parts that replace what wears out. Each listing says what it does, which iPods it fits, how to install it and where to get it.</p>

      <h2>The name</h2>
      <p>In Portuguese, a quitanda is the small neighborhood shop that sells fruit. You look over what’s fresh and pick what you like. The iPod came with one flavor. Here you choose your own.</p>

      <h2>How listings work</h2>
      <p>Listings are compiled from each project’s own public pages: its repository, release notes and website. Quitanda links to the project’s downloads and doesn’t host firmware files. Versions, dates and GitHub stars are refreshed automatically every night.</p>
      <p>Status follows the fruit, with the plain word beside it:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><span class="pill pill-stable">{STATUS.stable.ripeness} · {STATUS.stable.plain}</span> Its maker calls it stable and publishes releases.</li>
        <li><span class="pill pill-beta">{STATUS.beta.ripeness} · {STATUS.beta.plain}</span> Usable, still changing, or not yet released as stable.</li>
        <li><span class="pill pill-experimental">{STATUS.experimental.ripeness} · {STATUS.experimental.plain}</span> For the curious. Expect rough edges, and sometimes real risk to the iPod.</li>
        <li><span class="pill pill-archived">{STATUS.archived.ripeness} · {STATUS.archived.plain}</span> No longer developed. Kept for the record.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A listing here isn’t a guarantee. Read the project’s own notes, and back up your music first.</p>

      <h2 id="add-or-correct-a-listing">Add or correct a listing</h2>
      <p>Sign in and <a href={href("/suggest/")}>suggest a listing</a>: something missing, or a fix to one that's here. A person reads each one and adds it, and you can be credited below.</p>
      <p>The whole catalog is also one file in a public repository, so you can <a href={`${SITE.repo}/issues/new`}>open an issue</a> with the details, or <a href={`${SITE.repo}/edit/main/src/data/catalog.js`}>edit the file</a> and send a pull request. If you make one of the projects listed and something is wrong, any of these routes works, and corrections go in quickly.</p>

      <h2 id="thanks">Thanks</h2>
      <p>People who helped fill the shelves. Suggest a listing and tick the box to be credited, and your name goes here once it's in.</p>
      <ul id="thanks-list" class="thanks-list"><li class="aside-note">Loading…</li></ul>

      <h2>Shared themes</h2>
      <p>Anyone signed in can <a href={href("/themes/submit/")}>share a theme</a> for Rockbox, its forks or CrateDigger. Every theme is checked by a person before it appears in the <a href={href("/themes/")}>gallery</a>. Themes can’t contain programs, plugins or firmware, and they can’t include Apple’s fonts, icons or artwork. If you spot a problem with one, use “Report a problem” on its page.</p>

      <h2>Pictures</h2>
      <p>Screenshots come from each project’s own pages and are credited on the listing. If one is yours and you’d like it removed, open an issue and it will come down.</p>

      <h2>Who runs it</h2>
      <p>Quitanda is run by <a href="https://github.com/mrbarkan">mrbarkan</a>, who also makes <a href={href("/apps/cratedigger/")}>CrateDigger</a>, one of the library apps listed here. It’s listed on the same terms as everything else.</p>

      <h2 id="privacy">Privacy</h2>
      <p>You can browse and download without an account. Quitanda isn’t a business, but it shows a few small Google ads to help pay for itself, and it runs no analytics of its own. Signing in and the newsletter are optional, your data is never sold or shared for marketing, and you can download or delete it yourself. The <a href={href("/privacy/")}>privacy policy</a> has the details, and <a href={href("/gdpr/")}>your data rights</a> explains how to use them.</p>

      <h2>The small print</h2>
      <p>Quitanda isn’t affiliated with Apple Inc. iPod is a trademark of Apple Inc. Everything listed is made by independent developers and makers. Installing firmware or opening an iPod is done at your own risk.</p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>

<script>
  const h = (tag, attrs, ...children) => {
    const el = Object.assign(document.createElement(tag), attrs?.class ? { className: attrs.class } : {});
    el.append(...children);
    return el;
  };

  // Loaded when the list scrolls near, so the page itself stays light.
  const list = document.getElementById("thanks-list");
  const KINDS = { firmware: "firmware", app: "app", mod: "mod", other: "listing" };
  async function draw() {
    try {
      const { thanksList } = await import("../lib/suggestions.js");
      const people = new Map(); // one line per person, listing everything they helped with
      for (const s of await thanksList()) {
        const p = people.get(s.uid) || { name: s.name, items: [] };
        p.items.push(`${s.title} (${s.action === "fix" ? "a fix" : `new ${KINDS[s.kind] || "listing"}`})`);
        people.set(s.uid, p);
      }
      list.replaceChildren(
        ...(people.size
          ? [...people.values()].map((p) => h("li", {}, h("strong", {}, p.name), ` for ${p.items.join(", ")}`))
          : [h("li", { class: "aside-note" }, "Nobody yet. Be the first.")])
      );
    } catch {
      list.replaceChildren(h("li", { class: "aside-note" }, "The list couldn't load right now."));
    }
  }
  if ("IntersectionObserver" in window) {
    const io = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => { if (entries.some((e) => e.isIntersecting)) { io.disconnect(); draw(); } }, { rootMargin: "400px" });
    io.observe(list);
  } else draw();
</script>
